-- title:
--   Regular logarithmic supersingular-polar differentials lie in the image of Theta₀
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is an odd prime ($p \neq 2$), $M$ is a non-zero natural number with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, $H$ is a subgroup of $(\mathbb Z/M)^{\times}$, and $S$ is a set of natural numbers. Write $\Gamma' =$ [`CohCarrier.GammaH (M / p) (ModularCurve.infSubgroup p M H hpM)`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133), the congruence subgroup attached to the image [`ModularCurve.infSubgroup p M H hpM`](def/ModularCurve_XHDifferentialsModL.html#L246) of $H$ under reduction $(\mathbb Z/M)^{\times} \to (\mathbb Z/(M/p))^{\times}$, and $F' =$ [`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC K Γ'`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101) for the $q$-expansion function field over a field $K$. The submodule [`ModularCurve.ssPolarDifferentials K Γ' p`](def/ModularCurve_XHDifferentialsModL.html#L35) of $\Omega_{F'/K}$ consists of those differentials that are regular at every place outside the supersingular set [`ModularCurve.ssPlacesQExp K Γ' p`](def/ModularCurve_XHDifferentialsModL.html#L27) and have at worst a simple pole at each place of that set; [`AlgebraicCurve.regularDifferentials K F'`](def/AlgebraicCurve_RegularDifferentials.html#L26) consists of those $\omega$ such that at every place $v$ one has $\omega = f \cdot \mathrm{dCoord}_v$ for some $f$ in the valuation ring of $v$. Also $J_H(M) =$ [`ModularCurve.JH M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L127) is the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionFieldBar M H`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L123) over $\overline{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`.
--
--   The hypotheses fall into the following groups; the content of each is indicated, several of them summarised.
--
--   (i) Level data. `hHp` requires that every unit of $\mathbb Z/M$ reducing to $1$ in $(\mathbb Z/(M/p))^{\times}$ lies in $H$; `hin` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`](def/ModularCurve_XHOperators.html#L113), i.e. the Hecke input conditions `HeckeInputsHAlong` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ for every prime $\ell$, together with, for every $d \in (\mathbb Z/M)^{\times}$, the existence of an $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-algebra automorphism of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` which is a diamond automorphism for $d$. A unit $d$ is given with `hd`: the reduction of $d$ in $\mathbb Z/(M/p)$ equals $p$, and `hdH`: the reduction of $d$, or its negative, lies in [`ModularCurve.infSubgroup p M H hpM`](def/ModularCurve_XHDifferentialsModL.html#L246).
--
--   (ii) The Hecke algebra. $\mathbb T$ is a commutative ring, a $\mathbb Z_p$-algebra, acting on the Tate module [`TateModule p (ModularCurve.JH M H)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) (the inverse system of $p^n$-torsion elements) compatibly with $\mathbb Z_p$; `hfaith` says the action is faithful. The map `op` sends each generator in [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (the symbols $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \notin S$ with $\ell \nmid M$, $U_q$ for primes $q \mid M$, and $\langle d\rangle$ for $d \in (\mathbb Z/M)^{\times}$) to an element of $\mathbb T$ acting on the Tate module as [`ModularCurve.tateGenOpH`](def/ModularCurve_XHOperators.html#L99) does (`hop`), and `hgen` says that the $\mathbb Z_p$-subalgebra generated by the range of `op` is all of $\mathbb T$.
--
--   (iii) Local component and inertia condition. `S'` is an [`IharaLemma.IdempotentSplitting`](def/IharaLemma_IdempotentSplitting.html#L7) of $\mathbb T$: finitely many completely orthogonal idempotents $e_i$ and maximal ideals $\mathfrak m_i$ exhausting the maximal ideals, with $e_i \in \mathfrak m_j$ exactly when $i \neq j$; $i_0$ is an index and `hord` requires $\mathrm{op}(U_p) \notin \mathfrak m_{i_0}$. The valuation subring $\mathrm{Pl}$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ satisfies `Pl.LiesOverPrime p`, that is $p$ is a non-unit of $\mathrm{Pl}$. The $\mathbb T$-submodule $P_0$ of the Tate module is characterised by `hP0` as the set of elements of the corner submodule $e_{i_0} \cdot (\text{Tate module})$ on which every element of the inertia subgroup `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` acts through the cyclotomic character at $p$.
--
--   (iv) Coefficients and the action on differentials. $K$ is an algebraically closed field which is an algebra over $\mathbb Z/p$ and over the residue field of $\mathrm{Pl}$ (the latter being of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed); $\tau$ is a ring homomorphism from $\mathbb T$ to the $K$-endomorphisms of `ssPolarDifferentials K Γ' p` such that, by `hτ`, $\tau(\mathrm{op}(g))$ acts on the underlying Kähler differentials as the operator [`ModularCurve.genDiffModL K p M H hpM S g`](def/ModularCurve_XHDifferentialsModL.html#L266) (Hecke operators, Frobenius pushforward at $U_p$, diamond operators).
--
--   (v) Integral model and Néron object. `hj` asserts that [`ModularCurve.jqModC ℚ`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15) lies in the $q$-expansion field of the full modular group; $\mathfrak X$ is an `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`, a model of $X_H(M)$ over $R_p =$ [`ModularCurve.XHDRLevel.R p`](def/ModularCurve_XHDRModelAtP.html#L22) with its curve model $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; $\Lambda$ is a `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData` at level $M/p$ over $\mathrm{Pl}$, and `hrepΛ` requires that the designation built from $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$ and the unit section represents the relative sub-Picard functor of the level-$\Gamma_N$ model cut out by the fibrewise algebraically trivial condition `algEquivZeroCut`. $O$ is a [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`](def/ModularCurve_JHNeronObjectAtP.html#L53): a smooth separated group scheme $O.G \to \operatorname{Spec} R_p$ with commutative relative group law, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ from $J_H(M)$ to the generic points, Hecke endomorphisms and specialisation data.
--
--   (vi) Representability and Abel–Jacobi pins. The hypotheses `hD`, `hDQ`, `hsep`, `ajQ`, `kQ`, `ajbar`, `εbar`, `hpoinc`, `hajQε`, `hajQ`, `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, `hpts_law` and `hAJ` (summarised here) state: that the designation built from $(O.G, O.g)$ represents the relative sub-Picard functor over $R_p$ and, after base change, over $\mathbb Q$; that the base-changed curve is separated; that the generic Abel–Jacobi morphism `ajQ` is compatible with the zero section and pulls the Poincaré bundle back, at every point $x$ over a field, to the line bundle of the divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the zero section; the compatibility of the comparison morphism `kQ` with the two projections; that `ajbar` is the induced morphism from $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ to $O.G$ over the base, with `εbar` the chosen base point; that $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for the relative group law coming from `hD`; and that for all points $x, s$ of the curve model with $s$ the chosen base point there is a degree-zero divisor equal to the difference of the places of $x$ and $s$ whose class has $O.\mathrm{pts}$ equal to $x$ followed by `ajbar`.
--
--   (vii) The ring of inertia invariants. $R$ is a Henselian local domain with algebraically closed residue field, an algebra acting faithfully on $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, with `hRA`: $R$ maps into $\mathrm{Pl}$; `hRdvr`: $R$ is a discrete valuation ring; `hRirr`: $p$ is irreducible in $R$; `hRfix`: an automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lies in `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` exactly when it fixes the image of $R$ pointwise; `hRmax`: every element of $\mathrm{Pl}$ fixed by inertia comes from $R$.
--
--   (viii) The $p$-divisible group of the finite part. $\mathcal G$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup R p h`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) (levels with finite free Hopf-algebra structure, surjective transitions, $\operatorname{rank}_R \mathcal G_v = p^{vh}$, transition kernels the $p^v$-torsion ideals); $\Delta$ is an injective additive map from $\mathcal G$-points over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ to $J_H(M)$ with: `hΔlev`, that the level-$v$ points correspond exactly to $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$, the subgroup generated by the $p^v$-torsion classes whose Néron point satisfies the extension predicate `ExtendsToPlace` at $\mathrm{Pl}$; `hΔgal`, Galois equivariance of $\Delta$; `hΔhecke`, that each Hecke generator is realised by a transition-compatible system of $R$-coalgebra endomorphisms of the levels of $\mathcal G$.
--
--   (ix) The toric quotient. $\mathcal B$ is a [`PDivisibleGroup R p hB`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199) with maps $\psi_v : \mathcal B_v \to \mathcal G_v$, and `hhB`, `hhB2`, `hψt`, `hψker`, `hψsurj`, `hψred`, `hperiod` (summarised here) require: $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + h_B$ and $h_B = 2h'$; compatibility of $\psi$ with transitions; that a point of $\mathcal G_v$ restricts to the identity of $\mathcal B_v$ exactly when its $\Delta$-image lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$; surjectivity of the restriction on points; that residual triviality of the restriction forces residual triviality of the point itself; and that for $\sigma$ in inertia and $z$ in the $p^v$-torsion of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$, a point $y$ with $\Delta(y) = \sigma z - z$ restricts to a residually trivial point of $\mathcal B_v$.
--
--   (x) Embedding of the $p$-divisible group into the Néron object. A ring homomorphism $\rho_h : R_p \to R$ compatible with the embeddings into $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (`hρh`) and morphisms $\iota_v : \operatorname{Spec} \mathcal G_v \to O.G$ are given, subject to `hιbase`, `hιcl`, `hιp`, `hιpts`, `hιmul`, `hιt`, `hιhecke`, `hιfin` (summarised here): $\iota_v$ lies over $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\rho_h$; the induced morphism to the fibre product is a closed immersion; $\iota_v$ is killed by $p^v$; on $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $\iota_v$ recovers $O.\mathrm{pts} \circ \Delta$; $\iota_v$ is multiplicative for the relative group law; it is compatible with the transitions of $\mathcal G$ and with the Hecke endomorphisms $O.\mathrm{hecke}$ via a transition-compatible system of coalgebra endomorphisms inducing the Hecke action through $\Delta$; and the comparison morphism into the $p^v$-torsion of $O.G$ is an open and closed immersion whose image contains every point lying over the closed point of $R$.
--
--   (xi) Atkin–Lehner and local pins. `wgen` is a semilinear automorphism of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and `hwgen` requires that the involution $\mathfrak X.w$ of the model induces the action of `wgen` on places. A homomorphism $\rho : R_p \to \mathrm{Pl}$ compatible with the embedding into $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ (`hρ`) is given, with `hσA` identifying $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\rho$. The long specialisation pins `hsp` and `hspΛ` (each with many clauses, summarised here) state that for $i \in \{0,1\}$ and pairs of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points of the curve model with chosen lifts to the integral model inside the smooth locus, chosen points of the special fibre model above them, and a degree-zero divisor which is the difference of the two corresponding places, the class of that divisor has a Néron point defined over $\mathrm{Pl}$ whose specialisation is the prescribed glued class (in `hsp`, the admissible gluing datum with components the difference of the two special places in the slot determined by $i$ and zero elsewhere) respectively the prescribed class of the special-fibre divisor (in `hspΛ`). The pin `hdia0` requires that the diamond morphism $\mathfrak X.\mathrm{dia0}(e)$ acts on the places of the special-fibre curve model as [`ModularCurve.diamondActionModL`](def/ModularCurve_XHDifferentialsModL.html#L203) for the lift of $e$.
--
--   (xii) Frobenius, diamond and degeneracy operators on the special fibre. Additive endomorphisms $F$, $F^{-1}_{\mathrm{inv}}$, $F^{*}$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of `JHNeronObjectAtP.Fbar p M H hpM (ResidueField Pl)` are given with `hF`: $F$ is [`ModularCurve.qExpFrobeniusPushforwardModL`](def/ModularCurve_QExpFrobeniusModL.html#L243); `hFinv`: $F$ and `Finv` are mutually inverse; `hFstar`: $F^{*} = p \cdot F^{-1}_{\mathrm{inv}}$. A unit `pb` of $\mathbb Z/(M/p)$ reducing to $p$ is given together with $\delta$, acting by the diamond automorphism `diamondActionModL` attached to the lift of `pb` (`hδ`). Degeneracy data consist of $\alpha_i : J_H(M/p) \to J_H(M)$ and morphisms $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ over the base for $i \in \{0,1\}$, with `hpull` (compatibility on points), `hpullsp` (the specialisation of $\mathrm{degPull}_i$ is $(\mathrm{id}, F^{*})$ for $i = 0$ and $(F^{*}, \delta)$ for $i = 1$) and `hpull_mul` (multiplicativity for the relative group laws). Finally $\bar W$ is the endomorphism of $J_H(M)$ given by the action of `wgen` (`hWbar`) and `hUPgen` asserts the relation $U_p x + \bar W x = \alpha_1(O.\mathrm{degPts}_0 x)$ for all $x \in J_H(M)$.
--
--   (xiii) The Serre $d\log$ data. An additive map $\Theta_0$ from the $p$-torsion of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb Q}, \mathrm{xHFunctionFieldBar}\,M\,H)$ to `ssPolarDifferentials K Γ' p` and a map $\Psi$ from the same $p$-torsion group to $F'$ are given, subject to: `hrec`, $\Theta_0 x = (\Psi x)^{-1} \cdot d(\Psi x)$ as differentials; `hPIN`, for each $x$ there are a degree-zero divisor $D$ with class $x$, a non-zero $f$ in `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` and a Laurent series $y$ over $\mathrm{Pl}$ such that $p \cdot (\mathrm{wgen} \cdot D)(v) = \mathrm{ord}_v(f)$ at every place $v$, $f$ is the image of $y$ under the inclusion of $\mathrm{Pl}$, the reduction of $y$ to the residue field is non-zero, and the Laurent expansion of $\Psi x$ is the image of $y$ under the residue map followed by the embedding into $K$; `hMULT`, $\Psi(x + x') = c \cdot g^{p} \cdot \Psi x \cdot \Psi x'$ for some non-zero $c \in K$ and some $g \in F'$; `hNZ`, $\Psi x \neq 0$; `hORD`, $p \mid \mathrm{ord}_v(\Psi x)$ at every place $v$ outside the supersingular set; `hFIN`, the class $x$ lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,p$ if and only if $p \mid \mathrm{ord}_v(\Psi x)$ at every supersingular place as well; and `hP5`, at every supersingular place $v$ the differential $\Theta_0 x$ has a simple residue equal to the image of some element of $\mathbb Z/p$ in $K$.
--
--   Conclusion. For every $\omega$ in [`ModularCurve.ssPolarDifferentials K Γ' p`](def/ModularCurve_XHDifferentialsModL.html#L35), if the underlying element of $\Omega_{F'/K}$ lies in [`AlgebraicCurve.regularDifferentials K F'`](def/AlgebraicCurve_RegularDifferentials.html#L26), and if there exists $f \in F'$ with $f \neq 0$ and $\omega = f^{-1} \cdot d f$, then there exists an element $x$ of the $p$-torsion subgroup of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(\overline{\mathbb Q}, \mathrm{xHFunctionFieldBar}\,M\,H)$ such that, first, the corresponding class in $J_H(M)$ lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}\,p$ and, second, $\Theta_0 x = \omega$ (as elements of the module of supersingular-polar differentials).
--
--   The statement is the surjectivity half of Serre's description of logarithmic differentials in characteristic $p$, in the form needed here: every regular logarithmic differential in the supersingular-polar module is $d\log$ of a reduced $p$-th-root function attached to a $p$-torsion class of the finite part of the Néron object at $p$. It is used by the companion statement producing the $d\log$ preimage of a given logarithmic differential and by the count of the finite part `finPts` against the corner of the regular differentials, which in turn feeds the level-lowering step at a prime exactly dividing the level.
